    "case_id": "748",
    "case_summary": "A 76-year-old Japanese man presented with fever and back pain lasting 3 weeks after endoscopic treatment for common bile duct stones. Medical history included diabetes mellitus and hypertension. He was taking cilnidipine and metformin hydrochloride. Social history included smoking 20 cigarettes per day for 47 years and consuming 70g of ethanol daily. Height: 168 cm, weight: 54 kg, BMI: 19.2 kg/m2.\n\nTemperature: 37.8°C with normal vital signs. Liver was not tender and not palpable. No heart murmur, Osler's nodes, or Janeway lesions were present. No tenderness upon palpation of spine or bilateral costovertebral angles. No muscle weakness or sensory impairments. Straight Leg Raise test was negative bilaterally.\n\nLaboratory Tests:- CRP: 22.1 mg/dL (elevated)- WBC: 11,910/μL (elevated)- Platelet count: 10.3 × 104/μL (low)- AST: 15 U/L (normal)- ALT: 14 IU/L (normal)- ALP: 275 U/L (elevated)- Albumin: 1.7 g/dL (low)- Fasting blood glucose: 280 mg/dL- HbA1c: 9.3%- T-SPOT, HBs antigen, anti-HCV antibody, anti-HIV antigen/antibody: all negative- Tumor markers (CEA, CA19-9, AFP, PIVKA-2): within normal rangeImaging Studies:1. Abdominal CT   Image Description: Two hypodense lesions in the right liver lobe2. Abdominal Ultrasonography   Image Description: Echogenicity-mixed hypoechoic lesion without blood flow3. Enhanced CT and MRI findings:   - Multiple cavitated nodules in lungs   - Thrombosis of hepatic vein   - Low-density area of left-sided iliopsoas muscle swelling   - Enlarged high-signal areas in L4/5 intervertebral discMicrobiological Tests:- Gram staining: gram-negative bacilli- Cultures (blood, urine, and abscess fluid): All positive for identified organism",
